            <ArticleTitle>Discovery and biological evaluation of some (1H-1,2,3-triazol-4-yl)methoxybenzaldehyde derivatives containing an anthraquinone moiety as potent xanthine oxidase inhibitors.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>729-732</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0960-894X(17)30061-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.bmcl.2017.01.049</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A series of (1H-1,2,3-triazol-4-yl)methoxybenzaldehyde derivatives containing an anthraquinone moiety were synthesized and identified as novel xanthine oxidase inhibitors. Among them, the most promising compounds 1h and 1k were obtained with IC50 values of 0.6μM and 0.8μM, respectively, which were more than 10-fold potent compared with allopurinol. The Lineweaver-Burk plot revealed that compound 1h acted as a mixed-type xanthine oxidase inhibitor. SAR analysis showed that the benzaldehyde moiety played a more important role than the anthraquinone moiety for inhibition potency. The basis of significant inhibition of xanthine oxidase by 1h was rationalized by molecular modeling studies.</AbstractText>
